Subject: [PATCH] api: WebhookKeyPermission rejects anonymous with 401, not 500

The webhook-key endpoints (GET/POST /api/v2/{job_templates,workflow_job_
templates}/<id>/webhook_key/) are guarded by WebhookKeyPermission, which only
implements has_object_permission -> request.user.can_access(...). DRF's default
has_permission returns True, so an UNAUTHENTICATED request falls through to the
object check and calls AnonymousUser.can_access, which does not exist:

    AttributeError: 'AnonymousUser' object has no attribute 'can_access'

-> the endpoint returns HTTP 500 for anonymous callers instead of a clean 401
(the sibling detail view /job_templates/<id>/ correctly returns 401). The key is
not leaked (the error occurs before any response body), so this is a robustness/
correctness defect rather than a disclosure — but CTL-061 requires webhook keys
be reachable "only via the authorized endpoint", and a 500 is the wrong contract.

Fix: add an authenticated-only has_permission (mirroring IsSystemAdmin /
IsSystemAdminOrAuditor in the same module) so anonymous requests are rejected up
front with 401. Authorized behaviour is unchanged (authenticated users still go
through can_access for object-level admin).

diff --git a/awx/api/permissions.py b/awx/api/permissions.py
index ce42274..d0ca92a 100644
--- a/awx/api/permissions.py
+++ b/awx/api/permissions.py
@@ -256,6 +256,14 @@ class IsSystemAdminOrAuditor(permissions.BasePermission):
 
 
 class WebhookKeyPermission(permissions.BasePermission):
+    def has_permission(self, request, view):
+        # Reject unauthenticated requests up front: without this, an anonymous
+        # request falls through to has_object_permission and calls
+        # AnonymousUser.can_access (which does not exist), raising AttributeError
+        # -> HTTP 500 instead of a clean 401. Mirrors the other authenticated-only
+        # permission classes above (IsSystemAdmin, IsSystemAdminOrAuditor).
+        return bool(request.user and request.user.is_authenticated)
+
     def has_object_permission(self, request, view, obj):
         return request.user.can_access(view.model, 'admin', obj, request.data)
